ve wake and wake-reclaim orchestration\n229:     Owns:\n230:       - notify and turn-end wake chain starts\n231:       - startup wake reclaim sweep\n232:       - Tauri maintenance wake reclaim loop\n233:       - sweep disposition planning and recoverable candidate selection\n234:       - live-owner suppression before reaping or re-wake\n235:       - abandoned dead-owner debris marking\n236:       - auto-wake cap enforcement and consumed-notification suppression\n237:   - component: src-tauri/src/mailbox_delivery.rs\n238:     role: intrinsic-surface\n239:     Domain: mailbox delivery preparation\n240:     Owns:\n241:       - deliverable pending row filtering\n242:       - unconfirmed delivery retry suppression\n243:       - abandoned row suppression\n244:       - notification prefix and delivery nonce construction\n245:   - component: src-tauri/src/dispatch.rs\n246:     role: intrinsic-surface\n247:     Domain: cli_lifecycle_orchestration\n248:     Owns:\n249:       - startup wake reclaim sweep suppression for resume entrypoints\n250:       - top-level CLI dispatch sequencing\n251:       - runtime service dispatch selection\n252:   - component: src-tauri/src/run_tauri.rs\n253:     role: intrinsic-surface\n254:     Domain: tauri_runtime_bootstrap\n255:     Owns:\n256:       - wake reclaim maintenance-driver startup\n257:       - production runtime service construction\n258:       - Tauri command registration and app run boundary\n259: ```\n260: \n261: ## Residual Accepted For This Gate\n262: \n263: The sweep still has a fixed scan/start cap. #44 expands scan breadth and reaps dead-owner debris, but a pathological backlog can still require multiple sweep cycles. This is accepted because the normal single-leak path recovers immediately, #44 proves a recent recoverable leak is no longer starved by dead-owner debris under backlog, and abandoned rows are marked instead of retried indefinitely.\n\n(End of file - total 263 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"      - s